const HQ_6=[{"t":"harvey","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Harvey argues that the present phase of urban entrepreneurialism differs from earlier phases of civic boosterism. Statement II: In the present phase it is typically the local public sector that absorbs the risk of speculative projects, whereas in earlier boosterism private capital carried more of it.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Harvey suspects it is precisely 'this feature of risk-absorption by the local (rather than the national or federal) public sector' that distinguishes the present phase from earlier civic boosterism, in which private capital was much less risk averse (p. 7). So II is not just another true fact: it is the reason Harvey gives for I."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about the distinction between place and territory are consistent with Harvey (1989)? I. Territorial projects, such as housing and education, are designed mainly to improve conditions within a particular jurisdiction. II. Place projects, such as a civic centre, deliver their benefits to the residents of the jurisdiction in which they are built. III. Place projects can divert public attention and even resources away from the broader problems of the region.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and III match pp. 7–8. II is wrong: Harvey stresses that place projects make all benefits indirect and 'potentially either wider or smaller in scope than the jurisdiction', so they need not reach the local population (compare Baltimore's metropolitan effects with the purely local South Street Seaport)."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: the council of a Dutch port city agrees with a developer that it will supply the land, build the parking garage and cover any cost overruns for a new waterfront concert hall, while the developer collects the rents from the shops around it. The council presents it as a way to put the city on the map. Which of Harvey's concepts fits this arrangement most precisely?","o":["Competition for redistributions of surplus through central government","Speculative public-private partnership with the risk absorbed locally","Serial reproduction forced on the city by external coercive power","Managerial provision of services and benefits to the urban population"],"a":1,"e":"The council carries the risk while the private partner takes the benefits, in a speculative place-making project: Harvey's description of the entrepreneurial public-private partnership (p. 7). Serial reproduction is tempting but would require evidence that the project copies forms built elsewhere under competitive pressure; the case describes the risk arrangement, not imitation."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"critique","q":"Harvey treats inter-urban competition as an 'external coercive law' that pushes cities towards the lowest common denominator of welfare provision. Which criticism of this argument would an evolutionary institutionalist such as Moroni (2010) most plausibly make?","o":["Harvey ignores that symbols only work once inculcated in the mental maps of their audience","Harvey misses critical junctures after which reactive sequences lock in a land value pathway","Harvey overlooks that small groups with concentrated interests organise more easily than large ones","Harvey misreads competition between jurisdictions, which works as a discovery process testing rules"],"a":3,"e":"Moroni follows Hayek: institutions evolve through trial and error and embody dispersed knowledge, so competition among places is a way of discovering which rules work rather than a coercive race to the bottom. The other critiques belong to sociological institutionalism (Dembski & Salet), historical institutionalism (Sorensen) and Olson's rational choice, so they do not fit Moroni's logic."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which statement most precisely captures Harvey's position on the 'relative autonomy' of entrepreneurial urban governance?","o":["Local coalitions can experiment with their own strategies, but competition disciplines them after the event","Urban governance is in the last instance captive to capitalist class interests that prefigure its choices","Inter-urban competition fixes in advance which strategies a local coalition is able to choose from","Local autonomy has grown so much that capital accumulation no longer constrains urban governance"],"a":0,"e":"Harvey explicitly denies that urban governance is 'automatically (or even in the famous last instance)' captive of capitalist interests, yet argues that the coercive laws of competition force agents into line, with the forcing occurring 'after the event rather than before' (pp. 14–15). The third option shifts the timing from after to before, which is exactly what Harvey rejects."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Harvey argues that urban entrepreneurialism has made multinational capital more, not less, geographically mobile. Which causal chain reproduces his reasoning correctly?","o":["Public subsidies tie firms to the city that paid them, because leaving would forfeit sunk public investment","Lower transport costs make the qualities of places less important, so firms care less where they settle","Public risk-absorption and infrastructure lower the cost of relocation, so firms can move more easily","Local wage bargaining pushes wages up, so firms leave high-wage cities for cities offering subsidies"],"a":2,"e":"Because the public sector absorbs risk and provides infrastructure, 'the cost of locational change has diminished' for multinational capital, making it more mobile (p. 11). The second chain has a wrong link: Harvey argues that lower transport costs make small differences between places matter more, not less; the fourth inverts his point that local bargaining helps control labour costs."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A municipal policy memo argues: 'We cannot wait for national programmes. Together with business leaders and the university we will develop the station district ourselves, take on part of the upfront risk, and market it abroad as a creative hub.' Which reading offers the most fitting lens for a critical analysis of this memo, and why?","o":["Foglesong, since planning is insulated from democratic control to secure capital's collective interest","Harvey, since a coalition takes on speculative risk to construct a place that attracts mobile capital","Hyötyläinen & Haila, since a landowning city sells public land to whoever pays the highest price","Savini & Aalbers, since layered investors and SPVs detach land decisions from the local context"],"a":1,"e":"The memo combines a broad coalition (governance, not just government), local risk-taking and the marketing of a specific place: Harvey's three features of entrepreneurialism. Hyötyläinen & Haila is tempting because both concern entrepreneurial cities, but the memo says nothing about selling public land to maximise rent."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement is NOT consistent with Harvey's account of urban image production?","o":["Selling the city as a location depends heavily on the creation of an attractive urban imagery","An orchestrated urban image can counter alienation and so open mechanisms for social control","In Baltimore the image strengthened the power of the partnership that produced the harbour","In Baltimore local studies confirmed that the spectacle had reversed the decline of the city"],"a":3,"e":"Harvey cites local studies finding 'rot beneath the glitter' and Levine (1987) showing that the city as a whole was 'accelerating rather than reversing its decline' (p. 14); the image masked this. The other three are his own claims, including the political success of the image in consolidating the public-private partnership."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would endorse this claim? 'It is the welfare-state, managerial interlude of the 1950s and 1960s, not civic boosterism, that is the exception in the history of capitalist urban governance; urban competition goes back to the Hanseatic League.'","o":["Sorensen (2024)","Moroni (2010)","Harvey (1989)","Taylor (2016)"],"a":2,"e":"Harvey calls the welfare state and Keynesian compromise 'a particular interlude' and stresses that civic boosterism is an 'old and well-tried' tradition starting with the Hanseatic League and the Italian city states (p. 15). Sorensen is tempting because he also reads history, but his historical institutionalism traces critical junctures in Toronto's land value capture, not a Marxist long-run claim about capitalist urban governance."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a city region invests heavily in a fast airport link, a data and media hub and prestige office space with business schools nearby, hoping to attract the European headquarters of banks and media firms. Which of Harvey's four options is it pursuing most precisely?","o":["Competition for control and command functions in finance and media","Competition within the international division of labour in production","Competition within the spatial division of consumption and spectacle","Competition for redistribution of surpluses via central government"],"a":0,"e":"Harvey's third option is the struggle to acquire 'key control and command functions in high finance, government, or information gathering and processing (including the media)', which require airports, teleports, office space and specialised education (p. 9). The production option is tempting because infrastructure also matters there, but it concerns advantages for producing goods and services, not headquarters functions."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Foglesong argues that planning institutions in capitalist cities tended to be insulated from direct democratic control, for example through expert commissions and independent boards. Statement II: Foglesong develops his argument through the history of US planning from the colonial era to the 1920s.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":1,"e":"Both are true, but the historical scope of the book is not the reason for the insulation. Foglesong explains insulation with the capitalism–democracy contradiction: socialising control over land through a democratic state risks popular control going further than capital accepts, so planning is shielded from it."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims are consistent with Foglesong's account of planning? I. Planning is needed because congestion, incompatible uses and threats to property values cannot be solved by any single owner. II. Planning is best understood as a tool directly controlled by the capitalist class. III. The form planning takes reflects struggles between fractions of capital, property owners, reformers and working-class movements.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I follows from the property contradiction and III from his insistence that outcomes depend on the balance of political forces. II is the instrumentalist view that Foglesong rejects: it cannot explain why capitalists and landowners often resisted planning."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a city's planning commission imposes height limits and use zones that the city's largest developers fight hard, yet the rules protect property values and traffic flow for business in the district as a whole. Which theory of the state, as discussed by Foglesong, explains this best?","o":["An instrumentalist theory: the state is a tool directly run by capitalists","A pluralist theory: planners neutrally balance all interests in society","A structuralist theory: the state serves capital's collective interest","A market-failure theory: rules simply correct an externality for all"],"a":2,"e":"A structuralist account with relative autonomy expects the state to secure capital's long-term, collective interests even against individual capitalists, which is exactly what happens here. The instrumentalist option is the tempting Marxist answer, but it cannot explain why the most powerful developers lose."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"critique","q":"Which criticism of Foglesong's argument would a historical institutionalist such as Sorensen most plausibly make?","o":["Land's social character does not justify state control, since prices coordinate dispersed knowledge","Planning's form depends on critical junctures and sequences, not only on general contradictions","Small groups of landowners with concentrated interests organise more easily than dispersed tenants","Symbols and plans only change cities once their meaning is embedded in the audience's mental maps"],"a":1,"e":"Historical institutionalism explains institutional forms through critical junctures, reactive sequences and path dependence, so it would ask why planning took different forms in different places and periods despite the same contradictions. The other critiques come from Hayekian liberalism, Olson's rational choice and Dembski & Salet's sociological institutionalism."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which statement most precisely reflects Foglesong's view of planning under capitalism?","o":["Planning is not needed by capital, which only accepts it to legitimise urban development","Planning is needed by capital and therefore tends to expand until private property is replaced","Planning is constrained by capital, so in practice it never restricts what individual owners do","Planning is needed by capital yet limited by it, so it goes only as far as property allows"],"a":3,"e":"Foglesong sees planning as a contradictory response: necessary to socialise some control over land for capital's collective interest, but constrained because it must not undermine private property. The third option overstates the constraint: planning does restrict individual owners, which is why they resist it."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reproduces Foglesong's property contradiction?","o":["Land's value is collectively produced, so private control causes problems capital must socialise","Land's value comes from the owner's own effort, so public control must be kept as small as possible","Land is publicly owned, so private developers lobby to obtain more control over its use and value","Land is a scarce good, so markets allocate it efficiently and planning only adds transaction costs"],"a":0,"e":"Land's value and use depend on collective investments and neighbouring uses (its social character), yet it is privately owned; individual owners' pursuit of returns creates problems none of them can solve, so capital collectively needs some socialised control. The second chain gets the first link wrong, and the last one is a market-liberal argument, not Foglesong's."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would endorse this claim? 'Planning exists in capitalist cities because the social character of urban land clashes with its private ownership and control.'","o":["Foglesong (1986)","Needham (2012)","Moroni (2010)","Taylor (2016)"],"a":0,"e":"The clash between the social character of land and private ownership is Foglesong's property contradiction. Needham is the tempting option because he also links planning to property, but he sees planning as one way the state protects identifiable private interests in land, not as a response to a contradiction of capitalism."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about Foglesong's theoretical position is NOT consistent with the course material?","o":["He rejects treating planning as a neutral, technical pursuit of the public interest","He rejects the view that planning is simply a tool of the capitalist class","He leans to a structural view but leaves room for class struggle and contingency","He holds that capital needs planning above all to extend democratic control of land"],"a":3,"e":"This reverses the capitalism–democracy contradiction: capital fears that democratic control over land could go further than it wants, so planning tends to be insulated from popular pressure. The other three statements summarise his position between mainstream theory and instrumentalist Marxism."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: In the dispute over state land in the early 2000s, the City of Helsinki insisted on buying it at the price of raw land rather than developed land. Statement II: In Finland, cities hold the 'planning monopoly', the sole right to assign development rights, so the value increase due to development belongs to them.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are on p. 138, and the authors link them directly: Helsinki 'defended its right to buy the state land at the price of raw land' because cities have the sole right to assign development rights, 'hence the value increase due to development belongs to them'. II is the justification for I, not just a parallel fact."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about the motives behind the Eiranranta sale are supported by Hyötyläinen & Haila? I. Helsinki wanted to attract companies because corporate tax is the main source of municipal revenue in Finland. II. The city budget contained massive sales goals for the Premises Centre, so land and real estate had to be sold. III. Developers and companies argued that Anglo-American firms will not move to a city unless they can own the land.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"II and III are reported motives (pp. 140–141). I is wrong twice over: income tax is the municipalities' main revenue, which is why the city wanted 'good taxpayers', and footnote 13 notes that the state redistributes corporate taxes, so Helsinki loses that revenue."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch municipality moves all its buildings into an internal property company that now invoices its own schools, libraries and sports halls at market rent, so that 'the true cost of public space becomes visible and departments use space efficiently'. Which concept from Hyötyläinen & Haila fits most precisely?","o":["Shadow rents, calculated for city services but not actually paid","A development contract between the city and a private landowner","Entrepreneurial public real estate policy, market-rent form","Value capture by the city through its planning monopoly on land"],"a":2,"e":"Charging market rent when leasing is one of the two forms of entrepreneurial public real estate policy, here justified by transparency and efficiency as with Helsinki's Premises Centre (pp. 137, 139). Shadow rents are the tempting distractor, but they were only calculated, not paid, as a way of 'getting used to the market game' (p. 138); this case involves real invoices."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"critique","q":"Which criticism of Hyötyläinen & Haila's argument would a rational choice (public choice) theorist most plausibly make?","o":["The sale should be read as a critical juncture followed by reactive sequences of contestation","The name Eiranranta only works if its meaning is embedded in the mental maps of residents","The authors underplay how the state structurally secures the collective interest of capital","Discounted land lets organised insiders capture rents, so open bidding may curb rent-seeking"],"a":3,"e":"Rational choice and rent-seeking theory (Taylor, Olson) would stress that below-market allocation creates rents for concentrated, organised interests; the authors' own suspicion that established developers undervalued land even supports this reading, so a competitive sale could reduce rent-seeking. The other critiques follow historical institutionalist, sociological institutionalist and Marxist state-theory logic."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which statement most precisely reflects what Hyötyläinen & Haila say about Apollo's higher bid?","o":["Established developers had probably undervalued land to capture land rent belonging to the city","Established developers were shown to have formed a cartel that fixed their bids for the four sites","Apollo overpaid because, as a newcomer, it miscalculated the costs of the underground infrastructure","Apollo's bid proves that selling public land to investors always breaks the monopoly of developers"],"a":0,"e":"The authors offer as a 'plausible explanation' that experienced developers were used to undervaluing land to extract land rent and the value increase from the city (pp. 141, 143). The cartel claim overstates a suspicion voiced by one REC member, and the last option turns 'could lead to breaking the monopoly' into a general law."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reproduces how state-level changes pushed Helsinki towards entrepreneurial land policy?","o":["MRL strengthened state ratification of local plans, so the state could order cities to sell their land","The state redistributed corporate taxes to Helsinki, so the city sold land to attract more companies","State policy made cities compete and shifted welfare costs to them, so land became a revenue tool","The recession ended state real estate ownership, so cities had to buy state land at developed prices"],"a":2,"e":"Urban programmes made cities compete for subsidies, the 'necessity to save' shifted social services to municipalities, and so cities came to see public land as a strategic means 'to compete with other cities and to reap revenue' (pp. 139, 143). The first chain inverts MRL, which removed state ratification; the last inverts the recession, which actually made much real estate state property."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A council budget memo argues: 'Leasing valuable municipal land below its market value is a hidden subsidy that nobody can see. Selling to the highest bidder makes our costs transparent and puts our assets to work for taxpayers.' Which reading and concept offer the most fitting basis for a critical analysis of this memo?","o":["Harvey (1989): local risk-absorption in a speculative public-private partnership","Hyötyläinen & Haila: justifications that form a strong discourse naturalising market rule","Savini & Aalbers: land de-contextualised through layered investors and special-purpose vehicles","Sorensen: a land value capture regime shaped by critical junctures and reactive sequences"],"a":1,"e":"The memo uses three of the four justifications (put public assets to work, transparency, efficiency) and frames selling as self-evident, which the authors, following Bourdieu, call a 'strong discourse' that makes market rule appear natural (p. 139). Sorensen is tempting because land value is at stake, but the memo is about justifying the sale of public land, not about how a capture regime evolved."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about the Land Use and Building Act (MRL, 1999) is NOT consistent with Hyötyläinen & Haila?","o":["It ended the requirement that local land-use plans be ratified by the state","It legalised 'development areas' and development contracts with landowners","It made it possible to develop Eiranranta as a disconnected, separate enclave","It required each municipal land sale to include some social housing"],"a":3,"e":"MRL removed state ratification, legalised development areas and contracts, and 'made it possible to develop separate enclaves' (pp. 139, 142); the enclave thus had 'the blessing of the state' (p. 143). There was no such social housing obligation: Eiranranta was built without any social housing, against Helsinki's own social-mix policy."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would endorse this claim? 'What is perhaps unique in our case is that the state, by turning into just one player in the real estate market, lost its impartiality and with it the ability to manage the contradictions of marketisation.'","o":["Savini & Aalbers (2016)","Gurran & Ruming (2016)","Foglesong (1986/2003)","Hyötyläinen & Haila (2018)"],"a":3,"e":"This paraphrases the conclusion of Hyötyläinen & Haila, who contrast the Finnish state, which corporatised its real estate, with the UK state, which enabled others to treat land as a financial asset (p. 143). Savini & Aalbers also discuss public weakness, but theirs is a fiscally weak municipality dependent on project yields, not a state that became a market player."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a city in a country where municipal income tax is the main local revenue sees affluent families moving to villa suburbs across its border. It sells a prime waterfront for luxury apartments, explicitly to win these households back. Which concept from Hyötyläinen & Haila fits most precisely?","o":["Competition between cities for state subsidies through urban programmes","The 'necessity to save' as a strong discourse in the national media","Inter-city competition for 'good taxpayers' using public land as a tool","Serial reproduction of waterfront projects under external coercive law"],"a":2,"e":"REC members said a main purpose of the Eiranranta deal was to get 'so-called good taxpayers who now live in Espoo and Kauniainen' to pay income tax to Helsinki (p. 140). Competition for subsidies via urban programmes is also inter-city competition, but it concerns state funding, not residents; serial reproduction is Harvey's concept about copied project types."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Savini & Aalbers argue that the detachment between land and finance is on its own sufficient to explain the long-term vacancy of the Falck site. Statement II: According to the authors, such detached financial constructions are common to most financialised projects in western and non-western countries.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"The authors write that 'the detachment between land and finance is not a sufficient condition to explain a long-term vacancy like the Falck site', precisely because 'these constructions are common to most financialised projects' (p. 14). The second, decisive factor was the municipality's growing dependence on the project's yields, so I is false while II is true."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about special-purpose vehicles (SPVs) are consistent with Savini & Aalbers? I. An SPV isolates the risk of one urban project from the generator company or holding behind it. II. An SPV can be sold between holdings, as happened with Immobiliare Cascina Rubina together with its debt. III. SPVs are typically set up by municipalities to capture part of the land value increase.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are on pp. 6 and 14: SPVs insulate project risk, can be bought and sold, and in Falck were 'sold to the next buyers along with the debt'. III is wrong: SPVs are created by development consortia or holdings to manage investment; value capture is what local governments try to do through regulation, levies, land readjustment or TIF."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a chemical company closes a plant, transfers the site into a separate project company and uses the land as security for a large loan, which it spends on new factories in Asia. Nothing is built on the site for years. Which idea from Savini & Aalbers fits this most precisely?","o":["Capital switching into the built environment as a spatial fix","Clustering of separate projects into one larger investment package","Land used as collateral to create credit invested elsewhere","Investment relocation towards riskier out-of-prime locations"],"a":2,"e":"This is the step the authors say goes 'one step further than Harvey foresaw in 1982': land 'itself is used as leverage to create credit that can then be invested elsewhere' (pp. 14–15). Capital switching as a spatial fix is the tempting distractor, but there capital flows into land; here credit flows out of land to other sectors."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"critique","q":"Savini & Aalbers conclude that governing property-led development requires strong regulation combined with proactive public intervention in land development. Which criticism would an evolutionary institutionalist such as Moroni (2010) most plausibly make?","o":["The authors admit cities lack market knowledge, yet ask them to steer land more actively","The authors miss how audits, targets and bidding govern municipalities at a distance","The authors neglect how the PII regime locked negotiations into a path-dependent sequence","The authors ignore that gridlock reflects strategic bargaining with high transaction costs"],"a":0,"e":"Moroni, following Hayek, stresses that knowledge is dispersed and that public authorities cannot gather what they need to steer complex processes; the authors themselves note that small municipalities 'have a shallow knowledge of land markets' (p. 7), so more discretionary intervention looks risky, and Moroni would prefer general, stable rules. The other options follow governmentality, historical institutionalist and rational choice logic."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which statement most precisely captures Savini & Aalbers' view on whether the Falck outcome is inevitable?","o":["Gridlock follows inevitably under financial capitalism, as finance always overrides local planning","Gridlock is avoided whenever municipalities adopt stricter zoning that binds mobile capital to rules","Gridlock is mainly caused by municipalities that try to use financial markets for their own agenda","Gridlock is not inevitable; financial complexity and the public role in regulation shape outcomes"],"a":3,"e":"They state that they 'do not suggest that this is an inevitable outcome' and name 'the complexity of the project's financial constructions and the regulatory position of public authorities' as the factors that differentiate cases (p. 15). The zoning option is tempting, but they show that local zoning did not stop speculation and call for regulation combined with proactive capacity."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly describes what happened to the Falck programme each time the land changed hands?","o":["Each sale added debt and a higher land price, so the plan grew to promise more profit","Each sale lowered the land price, so the new owner could scale the plan down to fit local demand","Each sale brought the owner closer to local politics, so negotiations with the city sped up","Each sale gave the municipality more shares, so it shifted to an active partnership role"],"a":0,"e":"With each transfer the project became 'more bound to complex financial conditions of capital returns', the land price rose, and 'to realise ever-larger future profits, the programme of the Falck site had to be intensified several times' (pp. 13–14). Owners actually became more distant from local context, and the municipality moved from early public-private involvement towards a merely regulative role."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A consultancy report on a former harbour site argues: 'The value of the site lies in its expected yield. Bundled with two other sites in a single vehicle, it can be refinanced, sold on, or used to raise funds for other ventures; what is eventually built there matters less than the return on the package.' Which reading offers the best lens for a critical analysis of this report?","o":["Harvey (1989), since cities copy each other's waterfronts under coercive competition","Hyötyläinen & Haila, since a public landowner charges market rent to save money","Savini & Aalbers, since land treated as a liquid asset is detached from local needs","Foglesong, since planning is insulated from democratic majorities to protect property"],"a":2,"e":"The report treats land as a financial asset valued by expected yields, bundled and tradable, and even usable to raise funds elsewhere: the de-contextualisation of land capital (pp. 4–6). Harvey is tempting because waterfronts appear, but the report is about the financial treatment of land, not about inter-urban imitation."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about the history of the Falck site is NOT consistent with Savini & Aalbers?","o":["In the 1990s a development agency mostly owned by the municipality aimed to save jobs","Around 2002 a local investor's SPV proposed a modest plan with about 9% expected return","From 2005 Risanamento's Renzo Piano plan almost doubled the built surface of the plan","In 2010 Sesto Immobiliare paid the full price in cash, with no conditions for the city"],"a":3,"e":"The 2010 purchase for €405 million was largely 'a transfer of the land ownership to the banks involved', and it was conditional on the municipality approving a masterplan of at least 962,000 m² within two years (p. 12). The other statements match the periodisation on pp. 9–11."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"who","q":"Which course author(s) would endorse this claim? 'Because land is managed in portfolios together with assets elsewhere, decisions on its exchange and use are disconnected from the cultural values, normative frameworks and symbolic meanings of a specific place.'","o":["Dembski & Salet (2010)","Savini & Aalbers (2016)","Hyötyläinen & Haila (2018)","Harvey (1989)"],"a":1,"e":"This is Savini & Aalbers' core idea of de-contextualisation (p. 5), where they cite Dembski & Salet for the notion of a context made of values, norms and symbolic meanings. Dembski & Salet are the tempting answer, but their argument is about how symbolic markers acquire meaning through institutional embedding, not about land finance."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: to pay for a new tram line and park in a redevelopment zone, a city issues bonds that will be repaid from the expected increase in property tax revenue inside the zone. Among the value capture tools Savini & Aalbers list, which one is this?","o":["A levy that captures unearned betterment values from developers","A fiscal strategy such as tax increment financing (TIF)","Public-led land readjustment through a mixed corporation","Stringent regulation that controls the project's output"],"a":1,"e":"Savini & Aalbers mention that local governments 'can also issue project-bound bonds or use fiscal strategies to guarantee certain yields on projects (e.g. Tax Increment Financing)' (p. 7). A betterment levy is the tempting option, since both capture value increases, but a levy charges developers directly, whereas TIF borrows against future tax increments."}];
const OQ_6=[{"t":"harvey","fmt":"commentary","pts":15,"q":"Harvey (1989) claims that inter-urban competition works 'not as a beneficial hidden hand, but as an external coercive law forcing the lowest common denominator of social responsibility and welfare provision'. (a) Identify the theoretical perspective that grounds this claim and explain how it shapes his reading of entrepreneurial cities. (5) (b) Critique the claim from an alternative course perspective of your choice. (5) (c) What would that alternative perspective recommend to a city that wants to keep its welfare provision? (5)","model":"(a) The claim rests on Marxist political economy (historical-geographical materialism). Following Marx, Harvey treats competition as the 'bearer' of capitalist social relations: its coercive laws discipline firms, states and cities. Local coalitions seem autonomous and experiment speculatively, but the forcing occurs 'after the event rather than before'; projects that do not fit capital accumulation fail. This structural lens explains why cities of very different political colour converge on public-private partnerships, why risk is socialised while benefits are privatised, and why welfare provision is squeezed. (b) From historical institutionalism (Sorensen 2024) the claim looks too convergent and too structural. Outcomes are filtered by path-dependent institutions: national fiscal systems, land policy, planning law. Critical junctures open choices and reactive sequences of contestation shape what follows; Toronto's land value capture history shows that the distribution of costs and benefits is fought over and can shift. Competition is real, but it does not produce one lowest common denominator; Helsinki and Sesto San Giovanni reacted very differently. (c) A historical institutionalist would recommend using critical junctures (a fiscal crisis, a new planning law) to build durable institutions that lock in welfare goals, such as land value capture rules, public land ownership or ground lease, and requirements for social housing, so that later governments and competitive pressure cannot easily reverse them. Change is possible, but it is anchored in institutions, not in a single project.","rubric":["(a) Names Marxist political economy and links the claim to Marx's coercive laws of competition","(a) Explains the after-the-event disciplining of seemingly autonomous local coalitions","(b) Chooses a clearly named alternative perspective and gives a critique that follows its logic (e.g. path dependence, institutions filtering pressure)","(b) Supports the critique with a course author or case","(c) Gives a recommendation consistent with the chosen perspective, not with Harvey's"]},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"excerpt","pts":15,"q":"Excerpt from Harvey (1989), verbatim (abstract and opening, p. 3): 'In recent years, urban governance has become increasingly preoccupied with the exploration of new ways in which to foster and encourage local development and employment growth. Such an entrepreneurial stance contrasts with the managerial practices of earlier decades which primarily focussed on the local provision of services, facilities and benefits to urban populations. This paper explores the context of this shift from managerialism to entrepreneurialism in urban governance and seeks to show how mechanisms of inter-urban competition shape outcomes and generate macroeconomic consequences. [...] capitalists, like everyone else, may struggle to make their own historical geography but, also like everyone else, they do not do so under historical and geographical circumstances of their own individual choosing.' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this text? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the excerpt. (10)","model":"(a) Marxist political economy, in Harvey's version of historical-geographical materialism: urban change is explained by the dynamics of capital accumulation and competition, analysed dialectically. (b) Argument 1, structure shaping agency (a dialectical, Marxist view of history): the paraphrase of Marx's line that people make history but not under circumstances of their own choosing is applied to capitalists themselves: 'they do not do so under historical and geographical circumstances of their own individual choosing'. Actors, even capitalists, are constrained by the structures (including the built environment) produced by earlier rounds of accumulation. Argument 2, competition and the macro-economy as the explanatory core: the paper seeks 'to show how mechanisms of inter-urban competition shape outcomes and generate macroeconomic consequences'. The shift in governance is not explained by local preferences or ideas, but by the economic context (crisis since 1973, mobile capital) and by competition, which Harvey later calls an 'external coercive law'. The focus on 'local development and employment growth' rather than 'provision of services' also signals the political-economy concern with who benefits from urban policy. A further indicator is the stress on 'macroeconomic consequences': the city is analysed as part of the wider dynamics of capitalist accumulation (in the article, the transition to flexible accumulation), not as a self-contained unit of administration. This sets the text apart from rational or communicative planning theory, which would focus on decision procedures or dialogue rather than on capital and competition.","rubric":["Names Marxist political economy / historical-geographical materialism (5)","Argument 1 explained and supported with a verbatim quote from the excerpt","Argument 2 explained and supported with a different verbatim quote","Explains how each quote shows the perspective (structure, competition, capital accumulation), not just quotes"]},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"compare","pts":10,"q":"Compare Harvey (1989) and Savini & Aalbers (2016) on one specific point: who bears the risk of speculative urban development, and what that means for the public sector's position.","model":"Harvey describes the entrepreneurial public-private partnership as speculative in design and execution, in which often 'the public sector assumes the risk and the private sector takes the benefits' (p. 7). The novelty, he suspects, is that the local public sector absorbs this risk, which lowers relocation costs for multinational capital and diverts resources from collective consumption. The public sector is an active, risk-taking partner in a place-making coalition. Savini & Aalbers, writing a generation later about the Falck site in Sesto San Giovanni, describe a different arrangement. Risk is packaged and insulated in special-purpose vehicles that isolate project risk from holdings and can be sold on; land is used as collateral and the project is tied to layered investors and creditor banks far from the site. The municipality was too small to take on risk as an active partner, so it regulated and waited, but became dependent on the project's yields for public gains, which weakened its bargaining position. In short: in Harvey the public sector bears the risk directly as entrepreneur; in Savini & Aalbers private finance holds the risk in layered structures, yet the public sector is still exposed indirectly through yield dependence. Both conclude that planning becomes subordinated to capital (Harvey: the coercive law of competition; S&A: the need to unlock capital), but S&A reject inevitability and call for strong regulation plus proactive intervention capacity.","rubric":["States Harvey's position: local public sector absorbs risk in PPPs, private sector takes benefits","States S&A's position: risk insulated in SPVs and layered finance; municipality yield-dependent rather than risk-bearing partner","Draws a clear contrast on the public sector's position (active entrepreneur vs dependent regulator)","Notes a shared conclusion or S&A's rejection of inevitability","Uses at least one case detail (Baltimore / Falck)"]},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"apply","pts":10,"q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch city of 200,000 inhabitants wants to bid for a large international sports event. The council plans a new arena on a former harbour site together with a developer and a hotel chain; the city will pre-finance the infrastructure and guarantee a minimum number of events. Opponents argue the money should go to social housing and neighbourhood services. Analyse this situation with at least three concepts from Harvey (1989).","model":"First, the plan is a case of urban entrepreneurialism rather than managerialism: the focus is on fostering growth and image through a public-private partnership, not on providing services and benefits to residents, which is what the opponents ask for. Second, the arrangement is speculative and risk is absorbed by the local public sector: the city pre-finances infrastructure and guarantees events, while the developer and hotel chain take the benefits, Harvey's 'public sector assumes the risk and the private sector takes the benefits'. Third, it follows the political economy of place rather than territory: the arena constructs a specific place whose benefits are indirect and may be wider or narrower than the jurisdiction, and which can divert attention and resources from city-wide problems such as housing. Fourth, it fits the strategy of competing in the spatial division of consumption (spectacle, sport, tourism). Harvey would warn that inter-urban competition encourages serial reproduction: many cities build arenas and bid for events, so any advantage is ephemeral and the city risks debt (compare the New Orleans World Fair). He would also expect regressive distribution: subsidies to visitors and firms at the expense of collective consumption. Yet the image may still succeed politically, 'the circus succeeds even if the bread is lacking'. A Harvey-inspired recommendation would be to cooperate with other cities instead of outbidding them.","rubric":["Identifies the shift from managerialism to entrepreneurialism in the plan","Applies public risk-absorption / speculative PPP correctly to the guarantee and pre-financing","Applies place vs territory or the consumption strategy correctly","Uses serial reproduction, inter-urban competition or regressive distribution to assess likely outcomes","Uses at least three Harvey concepts accurately and links them to the case details"]},{"t":"fog","fmt":"commentary","pts":15,"q":"A planning historian argues: 'Early US zoning protected aggregate property values against individual builders, but it was placed in the hands of appointed experts so that tenants and workers could not use it to redistribute land values.' (a) Identify the theoretical perspective that grounds this argument and the concepts it relies on. (5) (b) Critique the argument from an alternative course perspective other than Hayekian liberalism. (5) (c) What would that alternative perspective recommend for land-use regulation today? (5)","model":"(a) The argument is grounded in Marxist political economy and theory of the state, as in Foglesong's Planning the Capitalist City. It relies on the property contradiction: land has a social character (its value depends on collective investment and neighbouring uses) but is privately owned, so capital collectively needs some socialised control over land, here zoning that protects aggregate property values against individual builders. It also relies on the capitalism–democracy contradiction: socialising control via a democratic state risks popular demands for redistribution, so planning is insulated through appointed experts. Behind this is a structuralist view of the state with relative autonomy: it serves capital's collective interest even against individual capitalists. (b) From a rational choice perspective (Olson, Taylor, Needham), the argument attributes intentions to 'capital' as a collective actor. Rational choice would ask which concrete actors organised and why. Owners of property in a district have concentrated interests and small numbers, so they can overcome the collective action problem and obtain zoning that protects their values; tenants are numerous and dispersed and do not organise easily. Expert boards can then be explained as rent-seeking by organised insiders (as in Taylor's Melbourne), not as a function of capitalism's needs. Needham would add that zoning is simply one way the state protects identifiable private interests in land. (c) Rational choice would recommend institutional design that lowers the transaction costs of organising for dispersed groups, makes the distribution of costs and benefits transparent, and clarifies property rights, for example tradable development rights or clear betterment rules, so that outcomes are less biased towards concentrated interests.","rubric":["(a) Names Marxist political economy / theory of the state (Foglesong)","(a) Correctly applies both contradictions (property; capitalism–democracy) and/or relative autonomy","(b) Critique follows the logic of a clearly named alternative perspective (not Hayek)","(b) Supports the critique with a course author or case","(c) Recommendation consistent with the chosen perspective"]},{"t":"fog","fmt":"compare","pts":10,"q":"Compare Foglesong and Harvey (1989) on one specific point: the 'relative autonomy' of the (local) state from capital.","model":"Both work within Marxist political economy and reject an instrumentalist view in which the state is simply run by capitalists. In the structuralist position that Foglesong leans towards, the state has relative autonomy from individual capitalists so that it can secure capital's long-term, collective interests, even against individual owners; planning socialises some control over land that individual owners resist. Its autonomy is limited, however, by the property contradiction (planning must not undermine private property) and the capitalism–democracy contradiction (planning is insulated from democratic majorities). Autonomy is thus explained by what capital collectively needs, and its form by the balance of class struggle. Harvey (1989) addresses the debate on the 'relative autonomy' of the local state differently. Entrepreneurial urban governance looks highly autonomous, and he denies that it is automatically captive to capitalist class interests. But he compares it to the autonomy of firms: local coalitions experiment speculatively, and inter-urban competition disciplines them after the event, as the 'bearer' of capitalist relations. So for Foglesong autonomy is a structural feature that lets the state act for capital as a whole, constrained by contradictions; for Harvey it is real freedom of local experimentation that is brought into line by competition between places. Harvey therefore adds a spatial mechanism (inter-urban competition) that Foglesong's account of the state does not centre on.","rubric":["States Foglesong's structuralist relative autonomy (state serves capital's collective interest against individual capitalists)","Mentions the limits from his contradictions (property, democracy)","States Harvey's view: autonomy like firms, disciplined after the event by inter-urban competition","Draws an explicit contrast (structural function vs competitive discipline / spatial mechanism)"]},{"t":"fog","fmt":"apply","pts":10,"q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch municipality wants to capture part of the value increase when farmland on its edge is rezoned for housing, and to use it for social housing and parks. Landowners and developers object, and the council considers handing the decision to an independent expert committee. Analyse the situation with Foglesong's concepts.","model":"The situation shows the property contradiction. The value increase of the farmland is socially produced: it results from the collective decision to rezone and from public investment in roads, transit and services, not from the owners' own efforts. Yet the land is privately owned, so owners and developers expect to appropriate the increase. Capturing part of it for social housing and parks would socialise some control over land, which is what the city (and arguably capital collectively, which needs housed workers and a functioning city) requires, and also what individual owners resist. Foglesong would predict that planning can go only so far as it leaves private property intact, so the capture is likely to be partial and contested. The proposal to hand the decision to an independent expert committee shows the capitalism–democracy contradiction: if land-value capture were decided by elected majorities, it could go further than owners and capital accept, for instance towards much larger redistribution. Insulating the decision in a technical body would let some coordination happen while containing popular demands. Foglesong would also ask about legitimation: an expert committee can present a compromise favourable to owners as the neutral public interest. Finally, he would stress that the outcome depends on the balance of forces between landowners, developers, housing associations, tenants and political parties, so organising those who benefit from social housing matters.","rubric":["Applies the property contradiction (social character of the value increase vs private ownership)","Explains why planning is both needed and limited (capture likely partial)","Applies the capitalism–democracy contradiction to the expert committee","Mentions legitimation or the role of political struggle in shaping the outcome"]},{"t":"fog","fmt":"position","pts":10,"q":"Place Foglesong on the reproduction–transformation dimension and justify your answer with his key concepts.","model":"Foglesong's account leans towards reproduction, with limited openings for transformation. Planning exists, in his view, because capitalist urbanisation produces contradictions that threaten capital's collective interest: the property contradiction (socially produced land under private control) and the capitalism–democracy contradiction (a democratic state might socialise land further than capital accepts). Planning's main function is therefore to keep the capitalist city working: it coordinates land use, protects aggregate property values and helps legitimise development as the public interest, while being insulated from democratic majorities through expert commissions and technical procedures. This reproduces capitalist property relations rather than transforming them: planning can go only so far as private property stays intact. At the same time, Foglesong rejects crude functionalism and instrumentalism. Because planning is a contradictory and contested response, its form reflects the balance of political forces between fractions of capital, property owners, reformers and working-class movements at a given time. This leaves room for transformation at the margins: stronger movements could push planning towards public housing or redistribution of land values, which is exactly the risk capital tries to contain. Compared with Marx (1844), who saw real change only in abolishing private property, Foglesong is more open to change within capitalism; compared with Moroni or Hayek, he sees planning as a site of class conflict rather than of rule evolution.","rubric":["Takes a clear position (mainly reproduction, with limited openings for transformation)","Supports reproduction with the contradictions, insulation and legitimation","Supports openings for change with class struggle / rejection of functionalism","Compares with at least one other course author"]},{"t":"hh","fmt":"commentary","pts":15,"q":"Hyötyläinen & Haila explain why REC members did not see Eiranranta as a warning sign of segregation: their focus on real estate business, and a 'strong discourse' of 'the necessity to save' that made market rule appear natural. (a) Identify the theoretical perspective that grounds this explanation. (5) (b) Critique the explanation from an alternative course perspective of your choice. (5) (c) What would that perspective recommend for the management of Helsinki's public land? (5)","model":"(a) The explanation is grounded in the political economy of urban land and critical theories of neoliberal urbanism (Harvey, Brenner and Theodore, Peck), with a Bourdieuian account of discourse. Land is analysed in terms of land rent and who captures it; the shift from leasing to selling is read as neoliberal marketisation and Harvey's 'neoliberal turn', restoring class power and converting common property rights into private ones. Decision-makers' views are explained by structural pressures (state restructuring, inter-city competition) and by an ideology that naturalises the market: 'Those are the rules of the game today'. (b) From an evolutionary institutionalist (Hayek, Moroni) perspective, the authors judge Eiranranta against an end-state ideal, a socially mixed, heterogeneous city steered by planners, and treat market prices as ideology. Moroni would argue that prices and private property convey dispersed knowledge that planners lack, and that leasing at administratively set, discounted prices produces opaque costs, which is exactly what the critics of the 'discount price' policy said. The authors' own evidence that established developers undervalued land suggests the old system was itself vulnerable to insiders, so the problem may lie in discretionary, case-by-case allocation rather than in markets. (c) A Moroni-style recommendation is kosmos-oriented: replace discretionary deals with general, stable and transparent rules for allocating public land (for example open auctions of long-term leases), and pursue social goals through general instruments such as housing allowances rather than through project-by-project steering of who may live where. Reform should be gradual and open to correction.","rubric":["(a) Names political economy of urban land / critique of neoliberal urbanism, with Harvey and Bourdieu's strong discourse","(a) Shows how the perspective explains REC members' views (structural pressure, naturalised market ideology)","(b) Gives a critique that follows the chosen perspective's logic (e.g. dispersed knowledge, price signals, end-state planning)","(b) Uses evidence from the article or another course author to support the critique","(c) Recommendation consistent with the chosen perspective"]},{"t":"hh","fmt":"excerpt","pts":15,"q":"Excerpt from Hyötyläinen & Haila (2018, p. 143), verbatim: 'What is perhaps unique in the Finnish case is that by establishing real estate companies and turning into just one player in the real estate market, the state lost its impartiality and therefore possibilities to intervene, \"to manage the consequences and contradictions of such marketization initiatives\" (Peck et al., 2009, 51). [...] The conversion of common, collective and state property rights into private property have been characteristic of neoliberal policies (Harvey, 2005).' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this text? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the excerpt. (10)","model":"(a) A critical political economy of urban land, specifically the literature on neoliberal urbanism inspired by Harvey's Marxist political economy (neoliberalisation as marketisation and dispossession of common rights). (b) Argument 1, marketisation as a contradictory, class-relevant process: the excerpt speaks of 'the consequences and contradictions of such marketization initiatives', borrowing from Peck, Theodore and Brenner's theory of neoliberal urbanism. Marketisation is not neutral efficiency, as the policy's defenders claim, but creates contradictions (here: exclusion and segregation in a welfare city) that someone has to manage. The state's turn into 'just one player in the real estate market' shows how the state itself becomes an agent of marketisation, a core theme of neoliberal urbanism. Argument 2, property rights and dispossession: 'The conversion of common, collective and state property rights into private property have been characteristic of neoliberal policies (Harvey, 2005)'. This is Harvey's argument about neoliberalism as the restoration of class power through privatisation of common assets; in the article it covers both the sale of development rights and the replacement of common laundries by private space. Together the two points show a perspective that analyses land in terms of rent, rights and class interests and treats neoliberal policy as the problem.","rubric":["Names critical political economy / neoliberal urbanism (Harvey, Brenner, Peck) (5)","Argument 1 explained with a verbatim quote from the excerpt (e.g. marketisation contradictions or state as market player)","Argument 2 explained with a different verbatim quote (e.g. conversion of common property rights)","Explains how each quote reflects the perspective rather than just restating it"]},{"t":"hh","fmt":"apply","pts":10,"q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch city that has long issued its land in ground lease (erfpacht) faces a budget gap. The alderman for finance proposes selling a prime waterfront site to the highest bidder, arguing that it will 'make our assets work' and attract high-income residents. Analyse the proposal with at least three concepts from Hyötyläinen & Haila, and state what risks they would point to.","model":"The proposal is a textbook case of entrepreneurial public real estate policy: a public body acting as a rent-maximiser by selling public land rather than leasing it. Its justification echoes the four arguments in the article, especially 'put public assets to work' (Sipilä's formula) and efficiency, and is framed by a budget gap, which recalls the 'necessity to save' as a strong discourse that makes selling seem self-evident. The aim of attracting high-income residents mirrors Helsinki's inter-city competition for 'good taxpayers', where land policy becomes an instrument of competition rather than coordination. Hyötyläinen & Haila would point to several risks. First, selling means giving up control: leasing kept planning power with the city, while a sale also privatises development rights, making it harder to implement future plans on the site. Second, a highest-bid tender equates efficiency with the highest-paying use, which tends to exclude social housing and contradicts a social-mix policy, as in Eiranranta, where no social housing was built and prices broke records. Third, the result may be an exclusive enclave that is not formally gated but socially closed. Finally, depending on who buys, selling to investors could start 'creating liquidity out of spatial fixity', turning the site into a financial asset. They would also ask who captures the value created by public development rights.","rubric":["Identifies the proposal as entrepreneurial public real estate policy (selling vs leasing)","Links the justification to the four justifications and/or the 'necessity to save' strong discourse","Applies inter-city competition for good taxpayers","Names at least two risks from the article (loss of control/development rights, exclusion of social housing, enclave, financialisation)","Uses Eiranranta details to support the analysis"]},{"t":"hh","fmt":"position","pts":10,"q":"Place Hyötyläinen & Haila (2018) on the structure–agency dimension and justify your answer with evidence from the text.","model":"The article sits between structure and agency, leaning towards structure in its explanation but giving visible room to agency in its narrative. Structural forces are clearly present: the early-1990s recession, state restructuring (closing the National Board of Public Building, founding state real estate companies), a new state urban policy that made cities compete for subsidies, the shift from welfare state to welfare cities, and the MRL. These 'scaled down' land-use decisions and pushed cities to treat land as a revenue tool, in line with the authors' neoliberal-urbanism framework (Brenner and Theodore, Harvey). Inter-city competition with Espoo, Kauniainen and other Nordic capitals adds a Harvey-style coercive pressure. Yet named actors make choices: Deputy Mayor Korpinen brought an 'economist's mindset', proposed selling to Apollo and even renamed the area; the Real Estate Department preferred Skanska; REC members unanimously supported the sale and only one councillor opposed it. The authors also criticise urban scholars, implying that beliefs and discourse matter and that people can be held responsible. Their method, interviews about policy-makers' ideologies, focuses on agency and meaning. So: structure sets the pressures, but the outcome is explained through actors who internalised a 'strong discourse'; this is a middle position, less structural than Harvey (1989), whose competition disciplines cities after the event.","rubric":["Takes a clear position on the structure–agency dimension","Gives structural evidence (state restructuring, MRL, competition, recession)","Gives agency evidence (Korpinen, REC decisions, discourse/ideology)","Compares with another reading (e.g. Harvey) or justifies the balance explicitly"]},{"t":"savini","fmt":"commentary","pts":15,"q":"Savini & Aalbers argue that in Sesto San Giovanni 'the project programming became functional to the urgent need to unlock capital for realisation, rather than being a tool to respond to particular local needs'. (a) Identify the theoretical perspective that grounds this argument and explain how it shapes their account of the Falck case. (5) (b) Critique the argument from an alternative course perspective of your choice. (5) (c) What would that alternative perspective recommend to a municipality facing a similar brownfield? (5)","model":"(a) The argument rests on a political economy of financialisation building on Harvey: capital switching into the built environment, and land as fictitious capital whose value is the capitalised expectation of future ground rent. Land becomes a liquid asset traded in portfolios, so investment decisions are detached from local needs (de-contextualisation). Planning then becomes instrumental to capital's need for realisation: the programme is inflated to cover debts and expected returns. The authors add an institutional element, the municipality's regulatory position and yield dependence, and reject inevitability. (b) From a rational choice / property-rights perspective (Coase, Needham), the gridlock looks less like the domination of finance over planning and more like a bargaining failure with high transaction costs and poorly defined rights. Development rights on the site were not fixed in advance but negotiated case by case under the PII regime; each new owner therefore had an incentive to bid up expected volumes, and the municipality, dependent on public gains from the deal, had an incentive to concede. Needham would add that planning is one way of protecting identifiable private interests in land; the issue is which interests get which protection, not 'local needs' versus 'capital'. (c) This perspective would recommend clarifying and stabilising rights before land is traded: a binding plan that fixes maximum volumes and the public contributions required (a clear betterment rule), so that the price of land reflects known rights and investors cannot speculate on future upzoning. Lower transaction costs through standard, transparent rules make negotiation shorter and less vulnerable to the financial condition of each owner.","rubric":["(a) Names political economy of financialisation (Harvey: capital switching, fictitious capital) and explains de-contextualisation","(a) Mentions the institutional element (municipal yield dependence / regulatory position) or the rejection of inevitability","(b) Critique follows the logic of a clearly named alternative perspective (e.g. transaction costs, property rights, dispersed knowledge, path dependence)","(b) Links the critique to concrete features of the Falck case","(c) Recommendation consistent with the chosen perspective"]},{"t":"savini","fmt":"excerpt","pts":15,"q":"Excerpt from Savini & Aalbers (2016, pp. 14–15), verbatim: 'In this paper we have demonstrated that the financialisation of land goes even one step further than Harvey foresaw in 1982: capital is not only flowing onto and through land, but land is increasingly also used as collateral (i.e., credit is not only used to invest in land resulting in spatial fix, land itself is used as leverage to create credit that can then be invested elsewhere). The possibility of realising future income streams through land is no longer merely seen as a fix for overaccumulation elsewhere, land is mobilised to create leverage, credit, and to seek profitable investments elsewhere. Under financialised capitalism, the full meaning of land as a form of fictitious capital becomes apparent.' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this text? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the excerpt. (10)","model":"(a) Marxist political economy, in the form of Harvey-inspired theory of the financialisation of land (the urbanisation of capital and the secondary circuit). (b) Argument 1, the vocabulary and problem come from Marxist crisis theory: the authors position themselves explicitly in relation to Harvey and his concept of the spatial fix: 'credit is not only used to invest in land resulting in spatial fix', and land is 'a fix for overaccumulation elsewhere'. Overaccumulation and the spatial fix are core Marxist ideas: capital that cannot find profitable outlets switches into the built environment. The authors extend rather than reject this framework, saying financialisation 'goes even one step further than Harvey foresaw in 1982'. Argument 2, land is analysed as a form of capital whose value lies in future rent, not in its use: 'Under financialised capitalism, the full meaning of land as a form of fictitious capital becomes apparent', and land is 'mobilised to create leverage, credit, and to seek profitable investments elsewhere'. Fictitious capital is Marx's term for claims on future revenue; the analysis focuses on capital circulation and accumulation rather than on planners' choices, ideas or rules. This explains why, in the Falck case, land use is subordinated to the need to realise returns.","rubric":["Names Marxist political economy / Harvey-inspired financialisation theory (5)","Argument 1 with a verbatim quote (e.g. spatial fix / overaccumulation / one step further than Harvey)","Argument 2 with a different verbatim quote (e.g. fictitious capital / leverage and credit)","Explains how the quoted concepts are Marxist (overaccumulation, fictitious capital, capital circulation)"]},{"t":"savini","fmt":"compare","pts":10,"q":"Savini & Aalbers cite Dembski & Salet (2010) when they define the local 'context' from which land is detached. Compare the two readings on one point: what 'context' (values, norms, meanings) does and how it changes.","model":"For Dembski & Salet, a sociological institutionalist reading, context is the institutional embedding of meaning: shared mental maps, norms and cognitive frames. Symbolic markers such as metaphors, landmarks or events can institute new social meaning for a changing city-region, but only if they become embedded in the audience's mental maps; designing a symbol is not enough. Context is therefore active and constitutive: it determines whether a planning intervention works, and change happens slowly through inculcation. Savini & Aalbers borrow this idea of context as 'systems of cultural values, normative frameworks, and symbolic meanings' (p. 5) but use it differently. In their political-economy account, context is what is being bypassed: financialised land is managed in portfolios and valued by simplified yield projections, so decisions on its exchange and use are disconnected from local values, norms and needs. Context here is relatively passive, a set of local demands that land use planning tries to represent, while the driving force is the logic of finance capital operating at other scales. The key difference: Dembski & Salet ask how meaning is institutionalised and changed through embedding; Savini & Aalbers ask how capital escapes local meaning. A sociological institutionalist might reply that financial calculation is itself embedded in institutionalised norms and frames (of investors and banks), so 'de-contextualisation' is rather a shift to another institutional context.","rubric":["Correctly states Dembski & Salet's view: meaning works through institutional embedding in mental maps","Correctly states Savini & Aalbers' view: financialisation detaches land decisions from local values, norms and needs","Identifies the different role of context (constitutive vs bypassed) or the different theoretical bases","Adds a reasoned evaluation or a link to the cases (e.g. Falck vs symbolic markers)"]},{"t":"savini","fmt":"position","pts":10,"q":"Place Savini & Aalbers (2016) on the deliberate–emergent dimension of change, and justify your answer with evidence from the text.","model":"Savini & Aalbers describe change that is mostly emergent. Nobody planned the fifteen-year gridlock on the Falck site: it grew out of a sequence of ownership transfers, each driven by financial conditions elsewhere, such as rising interest rates at Banca Intesa that led to the sale to Risanamento in 2005, and the credit crunch and Risanamento's debt that led to bankruptcy proceedings in 2009, events the authors call 'completely alien to the land use planning taking place locally'. With each transfer the land price, the debt and the planned volume rose, so the programme was intensified step by step, an unintended spiral rather than a design. The three trends they identify (relocation, clustering, instrumental planning) are likewise structural tendencies of financialised development, not deliberate policies of one actor. There are deliberate elements: Risanamento's clustering of Falck with Santa Giulia was 'a deliberate strategy of positioning', the 2010 purchase was conditional on approval of at least 962,000 m², and the municipality chose to regulate and wait. The authors also leave room for deliberate public action: the outcome is 'not inevitable', and they recommend combining strong regulation with proactive intervention capacity. So the account is predominantly emergent at the level of the outcome, with deliberate strategies by individual actors and a normative call for more deliberate public steering.","rubric":["Takes a clear position (predominantly emergent, with deliberate elements)","Supports emergence with case evidence (sequence of sales, external financial events, intensification spiral)","Notes deliberate elements (clustering strategy, purchase conditions, municipal choice, recommendation)","Explains why the balance falls where it does"]}];
const NQ_6=[{"t":"harvey","fmt":"recall","q":"What did the 1985 Orleans colloquium show, according to Harvey?","o":["A consensus that city governments had to become more entrepreneurial, disagreeing only on how","A split between left-wing and right-wing cities over whether to adopt entrepreneurial policy","A consensus that national states should take back responsibility for urban economic policy","An agreement among eight cities to stop competing for mobile capital and to cooperate instead"],"a":0,"e":"Participants from eight cities in seven countries agreed that urban governments had to be 'much more innovative and entrepreneurial'; the only disagreement was how (p. 4). Harvey stresses that the consensus held across national boundaries and even political parties, so the 'split' option is wrong."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"example","q":"Which example does Harvey give to show that the public sector does not always carry the risk in entrepreneurial projects?","o":["Harborplace in Baltimore, sanctioned by the 1978 referendum","The New Orleans World Fair and the city's federal dependence","The private risk taken in Gateshead's Metrocenter development","The joint tourism campaign of thirteen British industrial cities"],"a":2,"e":"Harvey notes that often 'the public sector assumes the risk and the private sector takes the benefits', but points to 'the private risk taken in Gateshead's Metrocenter development' as a reason not to generalise absolutely (p. 7). Harborplace is the archetype of the public-private partnership, not of private risk."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"inverse","q":"Harvey contrasts Baltimore's harbour, which could benefit the whole metropolitan region, with New York's South Street Seaport, which had only local impacts. Which idea does this contrast illustrate?","o":["Place benefits can be wider or narrower than the jurisdiction","Serial reproduction makes the advantage of copied projects short-lived","Command functions favour established centres such as New York","Image production gives residents a sense of place-bound identity"],"a":0,"e":"The comparison sits in his discussion of place versus territory: place projects make benefits indirect and 'potentially either wider or smaller in scope than the jurisdiction' (p. 8). Serial reproduction is tempting because both are harbour places, but here Harvey is making a point about scale of impact, not about imitation."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"inverse","q":"Harvey mentions Bologna and Emilia Romagna, where strong (communist-led) local state action promoted new industrial districts. Which strategy does this example illustrate?","o":["Competition for redistributions via central government","Competition within the spatial division of consumption","Competition in the international division of labour","Competition for control and command functions"],"a":2,"e":"Bologna illustrates the first option: creating production advantages, here through agglomeration economies and 'careful attention to the industrial and marketing mix backed by strong local state action' (p. 8). Rome and Bologna also appear under consumption (spectacle), which makes that the tempting distractor, but the industrial districts example belongs to production."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"not","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the consequences of urban entrepreneurialism that Harvey describes?","o":["Polarisation of jobs into low-paid and very highly paid positions","Greater instability and volatility within the urban system","Multinational capital that becomes more mobile between places","Rising collective consumption for the working class and poor"],"a":3,"e":"Harvey argues the opposite: public-private partnership often subsidises affluent consumers and corporations 'at the expense of local collective consumption for the working class and poor' (p. 12). Job polarisation, urban instability and more mobile capital are all consequences he describes on pp. 11–13."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"apply","q":"A left-wing council wants projects that serve working people, but its region competes with others for investment and jobs. What does Harvey expect to happen if this competition is zero-sum?","o":["The council can opt out, because a strong mandate protects it from competition","Even resolute municipal socialists end up playing the capitalist game","The council will gain, because socialist cities attract more mobile capital","Central government will step in and redistribute to compensate the city"],"a":1,"e":"Harvey warns that in a framework of zero-sum inter-urban competition 'even the most resolute and avantgarde municipal socialists will find themselves, in the end, playing the capitalist game' and disciplining the processes they resist (p. 5). He adds that under the right circumstances competition could open a non zero-sum pattern, but not by opting out."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"contrast","q":"Harvey states that 'socialism within one city' is not feasible. What does he propose instead?","o":["A return to national Keynesian welfare policy that makes cities dependent on the state","A geopolitical strategy of inter-city alliances to mitigate inter-urban competition","Withdrawal of cities from all public-private partnerships until capitalism is overthrown","A stronger role for charismatic mayors who can steer coalitions towards socialist goals"],"a":1,"e":"Cities remain 'important power bases'; the task is to devise 'a geopolitical strategy of inter-urban linkage that mitigates inter-urban competition', which he calls a progressive urban corporatism (p. 16). He does not call for a return to managerialism or a boycott of partnerships."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"recall","q":"How does Harvey connect urban entrepreneurialism to postmodernism?","o":["Postmodern planning theory caused cities to abandon managerialism in the 1970s","Entrepreneurial cities favour comprehensive modernist plans to impress investors","It favours design of urban fragments, ephemerality, and image over substance","Postmodern architecture made urban projects cheaper and therefore less risky"],"a":2,"e":"Harvey identifies a 'subterranean but nonetheless vital connection' between entrepreneurialism and the postmodern penchant for urban fragments rather than comprehensive planning, ephemerality and fashion, and 'image over substance' (p. 13). He does not claim postmodern theory caused the shift."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"example","q":"Which example does Harvey use to show that uneven growth can come from synergy between several entrepreneurial strategies?","o":["Sheffield, where a Labour council combined small-firm support and promotion","Baltimore, where tourism jobs rose from under one to over fifteen thousand","New Orleans, which combined federal dependence with a costly World Fair","Los Angeles-San Diego, where defence, command and consumption interacted"],"a":3,"e":"The Los Angeles-San Diego-Long Beach-Orange County megalopolis grew through interaction between governmental redistributions to the defence industry, command and control functions and consumption activities (p. 10). Baltimore illustrates the consumption strategy alone, not synergy."},{"t":"harvey","fmt":"define","q":"In Harvey's critical perspective, what is meant by the 'dual city'?","o":["A city split into a managerial old centre and an entrepreneurial new periphery","Inner-city regeneration surrounded by a sea of increasing impoverishment","Two neighbouring cities that cooperate to escape inter-urban competition","A city whose image and reality are always perfectly aligned with each other"],"a":1,"e":"Harvey says a critical perspective should look behind the surface vigour of regeneration projects to problems taking shape as 'a dual city of inner city regeneration and a surrounding sea of increasing impoverishment' (p. 16). The idea is about social polarisation, not about two governance styles."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"define","q":"What does Foglesong mean by the 'social character' of urban land?","o":["Land is legally owned by the whole community rather than by individual owners","Land's value and use depend on collective investment and neighbouring uses","Land should be allocated to social housing before any other urban use","Land owners have a moral duty to share their profits with neighbours"],"a":1,"e":"The social character refers to the fact that land's value and usefulness depend on streets, sewers, transit, what happens on neighbouring plots and the needs of the economy as a whole. The point is that this social character clashes with private ownership, not that land is legally communal."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"inverse","q":"Why does Foglesong reject the instrumentalist Marxist account of planning?","o":["Because it cannot explain why capitalists and landowners often resisted planning","Because it overstates the role of class conflict in shaping US planning history","Because it treats planning as a neutral, technical pursuit of the public interest","Because it ignores the market failures that planning is designed to correct"],"a":0,"e":"If planning were simply a tool controlled by capitalists, it would be hard to explain why capitalists and property owners often opposed it. Foglesong's contradiction-based account explains this resistance: planning serves capital collectively while threatening individual owners."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"recall","q":"How, according to the course summary of Foglesong, were early planning institutions shielded from popular pressure?","o":["Through referendums restricted to property owners and their tenants","Through expert commissions, technical rules and independent boards","Through direct control of planning by the largest local business firms","Through national laws banning municipal elections on land-use issues"],"a":1,"e":"Planning was often placed in the hands of expert commissions, technical procedures and independent boards, so it could serve capital's collective needs while containing democratic demands. Direct control by business firms would be the instrumentalist picture he rejects."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"define","q":"In the political-economy reading of Foglesong, what is the 'legitimation' function of planning?","o":["Granting legal permits so that construction can formally begin","Making informal settlements legal by registering property titles","Ensuring that elected councils approve every single land-use plan","Presenting capitalist urban development as the public interest"],"a":3,"e":"Besides coordinating land use, planning helps legitimise capitalist urban development by presenting it as serving the public interest. This is why a neutral public-interest view of planning is itself part of what the theory explains."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"not","q":"Which of the following is NOT among the episodes of US planning history that Foglesong's book is said to trace (colonial era to the 1920s)?","o":["Housing reform","The City Beautiful movement","The rise of zoning","Post-war urban renewal"],"a":3,"e":"The book covers US planning from the colonial era to the 1920s, with episodes such as housing reform, the City Beautiful movement and the rise of zoning. Post-war urban renewal falls outside that period."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"apply","q":"Since 1896 Amsterdam has kept much of its land in municipal ownership and issued it in long-term ground lease (erfpacht). How can this be read with Foglesong's concepts?","o":["As proof that capitalism has abolished private property in Dutch cities","As a way to handle the property contradiction by steering land publicly","As an instrumentalist tool by which developers directly control the city","As insulation of planning from capital by handing land to residents"],"a":1,"e":"Keeping land municipal treats its value and use as socially produced and steers them publicly, while buildings and use rights stay private: one way of handling the property contradiction. It does not abolish private property, and it does not hand land to residents."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"contrast","q":"How do Foglesong and Moroni (2010) differ in their view of private property in land?","o":["Foglesong: a beneficial evolved institution; Moroni: a source of contradiction","Both: a neutral legal device that planners can freely redesign at will","Foglesong: a source of contradiction; Moroni: a beneficial evolved institution","Both: an obstacle that should eventually be replaced by public ownership"],"a":2,"e":"For Foglesong, private ownership of socially produced land is the source of the property contradiction that planning tries to manage. Moroni, in the Hayekian tradition, sees private property as an evolved institution that embodies dispersed knowledge and should not be engineered away."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"example","q":"Which course case can be read as a concrete illustration of Foglesong's property contradiction, with landowners resisting public capture of land value?","o":["Taylor's Melbourne landowners fighting a betterment levy","Dembski & Salet's symbolic markers for a city-region","Scott's high-modernist plans for Brasília and Chandigarh","Cozzolino's anti-adaptive neighbourhoods built in one go"],"a":0,"e":"In Taylor (2016), landowners rent-sought successfully against the Growth Areas Infrastructure Contribution, shifting it to purchasers: private owners resisting the socialisation of value that collective decisions (the growth boundary) created. Taylor himself uses rational choice theory; the Foglesong reading is a course link."},{"t":"fog","fmt":"recall","q":"Where does Foglesong sit on the course's structure–agency dimension, according to the course material?","o":["Pure agency: planners freely design cities according to their ideals","Pure structure: outcomes are fully determined by the economic base","Structure-led, with room for agency through class and political struggle","Agency-led, with institutions emerging spontaneously from individual action"],"a":2,"e":"Foglesong stresses the contradictions of capitalism (structure) but insists that outcomes are shaped by struggles between capital fractions, owners, reformers and working-class movements. He explicitly rejects crude functionalism, so 'fully determined' overstates his position."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"define","q":"What were the 'shadow rents' that Helsinki's Real Estate Department began calculating after 1994?","o":["Rents secretly paid by developers to secure sites before public tenders","Rents for city services that were calculated but not actually paid","Below-market rents charged to social housing tenants on city land","Rents that the state charged the city for using former state land"],"a":1,"e":"Shadow rents were calculated, not paid, for the City's own services and administration; the authors describe this as 'playing and getting used to the market game' (p. 138). Only in 2005 did the Premises Centre start charging actual market rent."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"recall","q":"In the price dispute over state land in the Helsinki region, what did the parties demand?","o":["The state wanted raw-land prices, the city wanted to pay developed-land prices","Both agreed on market value, but disagreed about who should pay remediation","The state wanted developed-land prices, the city insisted on raw-land prices","The city wanted the land for free, the state offered a long-term ground lease"],"a":2,"e":"The state real estate company demanded the price of developed land, while Helsinki defended its right to buy at the price of raw land because the planning monopoly makes the value increase the city's (p. 138). The authors use this to show the state behaving as a rent-maximising market player."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"define","q":"What is a development contract in the sense of the Finnish Land Use and Building Act (MRL)?","o":["A lease that lets a municipality keep control of land use in the future","A contract between a municipality and a private landowner about a plan","A state subsidy contract awarded to cities with the best urban programme","An agreement between developers to divide the market among themselves"],"a":1,"e":"A development contract is 'a contract between a private landowner and a municipality regarding the plan and its implementation' (p. 139). Critics recalled that such contracts in the 1960s and 1970s had produced poor-quality, peripheral housing estates."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"recall","q":"Why was the former depot area renamed 'Eiranranta'?","o":["To honour the working-class history of neighbouring Punavuori","To mark the city's decision to keep the land in public ownership","To brand the area by linking it to the prestigious district Eira","To meet a state requirement that development areas be renamed"],"a":2,"e":"REC members recalled that 'the name was changed to Eiranranta with marketing in mind', an idea of Deputy Mayor Korpinen, connecting it to the old prestigious neighbourhood Eira (p. 140). The authors later note that the discourse emphasised prestige rather than stigma."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"not","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three changes that, according to the authors, 'scaled down' land-use decisions to Finnish cities?","o":["A new state urban policy that made cities compete via urban programmes","A new redistribution policy shifting social services to the municipalities","The Land Use and Building Act (MRL) of 1999 ending state ratification","A national rule obliging cities to sell their land to the highest bidder"],"a":3,"e":"The three changes were the new state urban policy, the new redistribution policy under 'the necessity to save', and MRL (pp. 138–139). No national rule required sale to the highest bidder; the highest-offer rule was set by Helsinki itself for the Eiranranta tender."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"apply","q":"The Eiranranta tender stated that the highest offer would win, provided the bidder showed exceptional building expertise. What does this reveal, according to the authors, about what 'efficiency' meant in practice?","o":["That land should be used for its highest-paying alternative","That land should be allocated to the most experienced builders","That land should serve the widest possible mix of residents","That land should be developed as quickly as possible by anyone"],"a":0,"e":"The authors write that the highest-offer rule 'gives a clue as to what the Premises Centre means by efficiency: the land should be used in accordance with its highest paying alternative' (p. 140). The expertise condition was a reservation added by the Council, not the definition of efficiency."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"contrast","q":"Hyötyläinen & Haila compare Helsinki's 'discursive practice' with the neoliberal redevelopment projects described by Weber (2002). What is the difference?","o":["Helsinki stigmatised the site as derelict to justify clearing it","Helsinki presented the site as prestigious, not as stigmatised","Helsinki avoided any public discourse and sold the land in secret","Helsinki framed the site as social housing to win council approval"],"a":1,"e":"Unlike projects that 'stigmatize properties targeted for demolition and redevelopment', Helsinki's discourse emphasised Eiranranta as prestigious, with luxury housing and good taxpayers who would benefit the whole city (p. 144). The sale was in fact publicised, including the record prices."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"example","q":"The authors quote a Finnish urban scholar who said that 'a good city can have different types of areas, luxury ones and rough ones'. Why do they cite this?","o":["To show that scholars celebrating diversity end up justifying privatisation","To show that academic research proved social mixing policy to be ineffective","To show that urban scholars opposed the sale of Eiranranta from the start","To show that the state used academic advice to draft the MRL legislation"],"a":0,"e":"The authors argue that scholars who celebrate free choice and diversity of neighbourhoods 'accept the privatization of public lands', justifying a two-sided policy: let some areas become rough and develop owner-occupied housing on privatised land (pp. 139–140). They call on scholars to take responsibility for such beliefs."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"recall","q":"On what kinds of data do Hyötyläinen & Haila base their study of Eiranranta?","o":["A survey of Eiranranta residents and a hedonic analysis of prices","Council and committee documents, REC interviews and field visits","Interviews with developers and investors plus company accounts","National statistics on land sales in all Finnish municipalities"],"a":1,"e":"They analysed agendas and minutes of the City Council and Real Estate Committee, interviewed five of the nine REC members who decided the sale, and used observations and photographs from field visits (pp. 137–138). They did not survey residents or analyse company accounts."},{"t":"hh","fmt":"define","q":"REC members described land policy in the 2000s as being about 'coordination'. What did this word now mean, according to the authors?","o":["Settling conflicts and pursuing balanced development between areas","Joint planning of social housing by all metropolitan municipalities","Aligning city plans with the national government's urban programme","Using land policy as an instrument to compete with other cities"],"a":3,"e":"The authors stress that 'coordination' here meant that land policy was 'an instrument to compete with other cities of the metropolitan area', not coordination in the old sense of settling conflicts and pursuing balanced development under the 1958 planning law (p. 140)."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"recall","q":"Why, according to Savini & Aalbers, are redevelopment projects on peripheral brownfields riskier than downtown projects?","o":["Because national law forbids new housing on former industrial land","Because such sites are always owned by many small landowners at once","Because of remediation costs, weak land pressure and poor infrastructure","Because local residents in urban belts oppose all new development there"],"a":2,"e":"Out-of-prime sites carry 'high initial costs for soil remediation and development, a weaker land pressure due to lower urban densities, and a lack of major infrastructure', yet promise higher long-term yields (p. 3). This makes them suitable for large consortia that can repackage risk."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"define","q":"What do Savini & Aalbers mean when they say clustered projects are treated as 'creators of growth'?","o":["Projects are meant to activate markets rather than meet local demand","Projects are designed to create jobs for the local unemployed workforce","Projects grow step by step through incremental, self-organised change","Projects are expanded only once local demand has been firmly proven"],"a":0,"e":"Clustering treats projects as 'tools of activation for real estate markets in so-called underperforming areas, rather than as a response to the emerging residential and entrepreneurial demands of these localities' (p. 3). Marketing and lobbying for public investment are then used to increase demand."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"recall","q":"In the 2000–2005 phase, when a local investor owned the Falck site through the SPV Immobiliare Cascina Rubina, what did negotiations with the municipality mainly concern?","o":["The share of social housing demanded by residents' groups","The jobs the project would generate during restructuring","The heritage protection demanded by the regional government","The bonds the city would issue to finance the infrastructure"],"a":1,"e":"Negotiations were managed directly between the owner and the Deputy Mayor and 'mostly regarded the extent to which the project would generate jobs in times of economic restructuring' (p. 10). This illustrates a still territorially bounded financial construction."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"recall","q":"Why did the city council not approve the plan proposed in 2002 for the Falck site?","o":["Its programme had too much high-range office space and vague industry","The investor withdrew after the credit crunch and the bankruptcy case","The region refused to fund the health campus the plan depended on","Residents won a legal appeal against the soil remediation strategy"],"a":0,"e":"The 2002 plan was never approved 'because of the programme of excessive high-range office space and undefined amounts of industrial activity'; the city imposed a restrictive land use plan and demanded more certainty (p. 10). The credit crunch and the health campus belong to later phases."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"recall","q":"How did Risanamento acquire the SPV that owned the Falck site in 2005?","o":["With a large cash payment funded by the profits of its industrial branch","Through a municipal tender that it won with the highest public bid","With little capital: a small down payment, inherited debt and new stock","Through a merger forced by the Milan public prosecutor's office in court"],"a":2,"e":"Risanamento bought the company 'without significant capital', with a small down payment to the former owner, inheriting the debt to Banca Intesa, and issuing stocks for 25% of the capitalised value (p. 11). The prosecutor came in only in 2009, with the bankruptcy procedure."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"not","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the developments that, according to Savini & Aalbers, institutionalised the cycle of production, leverage and exchange in real estate?","o":["The availability of so-called 'cheap money'","Guarantees on long-term investments in land","Regulation promoting land economies of scale","Stricter land-use rules for large projects"],"a":3,"e":"The authors list cheap money, a regulatory framework promoting land economies of scale, guarantees on long-term investments and 'more flexible land use regulations for large-scale projects' (p. 3). Stricter rules is the reverse of what they describe."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"apply","q":"Sesto San Giovanni 'regulated and waited' instead of entering an active partnership in the Falck project. Why, according to the authors?","o":["Italian law forbids municipalities from owning shares in developers","For a small municipality, partnership would hugely raise public risk","The city expected that regulation alone would maximise value capture","The region had already promised to take over the risk of the project"],"a":1,"e":"For 'a small peripheral municipality, opting for a proactive partnership in the project development was not a viable option, as it would have dramatically increased the public risk in a gigantic project' (p. 14). The authors show that this regulative role then progressively weakened the city's position."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"define","q":"Savini & Aalbers quote Harvey (1982) on land as fictitious capital. What does this idea mean?","o":["Land prices are invented by planners and have no basis in any real demand","Land is worthless until a building has actually been constructed on it","Land owned by the state cannot be bought or sold on financial markets","What is traded is title to future ground rent, an interest-bearing asset"],"a":3,"e":"Under financialisation, 'what is bought and sold is not the land, but title to the ground rent yielded by it. The money laid out is equivalent to an interest-bearing investment' (Harvey 1982, quoted p. 14). The authors add that only now does 'the full meaning of land as a form of fictitious capital' become apparent."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"contrast","q":"How do Savini & Aalbers contrast developers of large urban projects in the mid-1980s with those of today?","o":["Then locally embedded specialists; now often outsiders to the locality","Then many small public firms; now a few large municipal companies","Then mainly foreign investors; now mostly local, family-owned firms","Then dependent on public subsidy; now financed only by their own equity"],"a":0,"e":"In the mid-1980s 'highly specialised, locally embedded developers' dominated; today developers 'can be completely extraneous to the local context' and rely on advisors and consultants, combining capital from different sources (p. 5). This growing distance is part of de-contextualisation."},{"t":"savini","fmt":"inverse","q":"In 2011 the council approved the Sesto Immobiliare masterplan, helped by the regional government's €323 million investment in a health and research campus on the site. Which of the authors' trends does this illustrate best?","o":["Investment relocation from downtown to peripheral areas","Instrumental planning used to unlock a stuck project","Clustering of separate projects into a single holding","Value capture through a betterment levy on developers"],"a":1,"e":"Public tools and money were mobilised to activate a financially stuck project, so planning became 'functional to the urgent need to unlock capital for realisation, rather than being a tool to respond to particular local needs' (p. 14). Relocation describes where investment goes, not how public planning responds."}];
